<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-2" name=3D"story-2" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>2. OIG exposes a $587.7 million P=
art D reference-data failure</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">HHS-OIG reported on August 31 that Par=
t D sponsors made $587.7 million of ineligible payments from 2021 through 202=
3 for five drugs that remained labeled prescription-only after their brand-na=
me equivalents became available over the counter. OIG attributed the failure =
to obsolete FDA information in CMS=E2=80=99s Part D Formulary Reference File =
and CMS=E2=80=99s lack of a deadline for sponsors to reject claims after an R=
x-to-OTC switch.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS concurred with OIG=E2=80=99s recom=
mendation to establish a rejection timeframe. OIG did <strong>not</strong> re=
commend recovering the full $587.7 million, so the estimate should not be tre=
ated as a disclosed sponsor repayment liability. <a href=3D"https://oig.hhs.g=
ov/reports/all/2026/cms-oversight-did-not-prevent-medicare-part-d-sponsors-fr=
om-making-5877-million-in-ineligible-payments-to-pharmacies-for-drugs-availab=
le-over-the-counter-but-labeled-as-prescription-only/" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a=
;text-decoration:underline;">HHS-OIG, =E2=80=9CCMS Oversight Did Not Prevent =
Medicare Part D Sponsors From Making $587.7 Million in Ineligible Payments,=
=E2=80=9D August 31, 2026</a>. (<a href=3D"https://www.oig.hhs.gov/reports/al=
l/2026/cms-oversight-did-not-prevent-medicare-part-d-sponsors-from-making-587=
7-million-in-ineligible-payments-to-pharmacies-for-drugs-available-over-the-c=
ounter-but-labeled-as-prescription-only/" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decorat=
ion:underline;">oig.hhs.gov</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">This qualifies last week=E2=80=99s evi=
dence that CMS is moving payment integrity toward prepayment intervention. So=
phisticated anomaly models cannot compensate for incorrect product-status dat=
a embedded in the adjudication stack.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Part D plans and PBMs need tighter syn=
chronization across FDA labeling, CMS formulary files, National Drug Codes an=
d point-of-sale edits. The commercial opportunity favors vendors that combine=
 regulatory reference-data management with configurable claims rules, excepti=
on handling and audit trails=E2=80=94not analytics alone. Sponsors face remed=
iation costs and prospective leakage reduction, but a reliable public estimat=
e of direct plan-level exposure is unavailable.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">CMS=E2=80=99s rejection deadline; whet=
her sponsors must review historical prescription-drug-event records; and whet=
her OIG expands the methodology to terminated NDCs, excluded products or othe=
r formulary-status mismatches.</p>
<hr style=3D"border:0;border-top:1px solid #d5dde3;margin:18px 0;"/>
<h3 style=3D"font-family:Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;font-size:17px;col=
or:#12395b;margin:22px 0 8px;"><a id=3D"story-3" name=3D"story-3" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;"></a>3. Health Net=E2=80=99s exit rein=
forces the structural thinning of traditional group insurance</h3>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>What happened</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Health Net began notifying employers a=
nd brokers on September 1 that it will exit traditional small- and large-grou=
p HMO, PPO and POS products in California and Oregon. Coverage will generally=
 end February 28, 2027. Centene said the decision does not affect Marketplace=
, Medicaid or Medicare products and reflects a decision to focus on governmen=
t-sponsored coverage. <a href=3D"https://www.beckerspayer.com/payer/centenes-=
health-net-to-exit-commercial-group-market-in-california-oregon/" style=3D"co=
lor:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">Becker=E2=80=99s Payer Issues, =E2=80=
=9CCentene=E2=80=99s Health Net to Exit Commercial Group Market in California=
, Oregon,=E2=80=9D September 2, 2026</a>. (<a href=3D"https://www.beckerspaye=
r.com/payer/centenes-health-net-to-exit-commercial-group-market-in-california=
-oregon/" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">beckerspayer.com=
</a>)</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Separately, CBO=E2=80=99s new small-bu=
siness market analysis found that only 53% of small-business employees worked=
 for firms offering coverage in 2024. Enrollment in small-business plans grew=
 10% from 2014 to 2024 while small-business employment grew 21%. Community-ra=
ted products fell to 50% of enrollment, while self-insured and other non-comm=
unity-rated arrangements reached 46%, driven largely by level-funded plans. C=
BO said migration by healthier groups may have worsened risk in the community=
-rated pool, although multiple factors affect premiums. <a href=3D"https://ww=
w.cbo.gov/publication/62745" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline=
;">Congressional Budget Office, =E2=80=9CHealth Insurance Options for Small B=
usinesses,=E2=80=9D September 2026</a>. (<a href=3D"https://www.cbo.gov/publi=
cation/62745" style=3D"color:#1a5e9a;text-decoration:underline;">cbo.gov</a>)=
</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Implication</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">The combined evidence points to segmen=
tation rather than a simple contraction of employer coverage. Carriers can pr=
ioritize Medicaid, Medicare and ACA markets while healthier or more predictab=
le employer groups migrate toward level funding, stop-loss and other experien=
ce-sensitive arrangements.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">That creates demand for underwriting d=
ata, stop-loss administration, benefit-navigation and ICHRA infrastructure, b=
ut potentially weakens community-rated risk pools. Providers should expect pa=
yer-mix migration and contract-transition work rather than assume affected me=
mbers will become uninsured. State-specific membership and competitive-share =
effects remain publicly unavailable.</p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;"><strong>Watch next</strong></p>
<p style=3D"font-family:Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;font-size:15px;line-heig=
ht:1.5;color:#1f2933;margin:0 0 12px;">Health Net=E2=80=99s regulatory filing=
s; replacement-carrier decisions during employer renewals; and whether other =
regional carriers reduce traditional group exposure for 2027.</p>
